{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-24-06j-spring-2009", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["24.06J"], "cross_listed_codes": ["STS.006J"], "course_title": "Bioethics", "course_slug": "24-06j-bioethics-spring-2009", "term": "Spring", "year": 2009, "as_taught_in": "Spring 2009", "language": "en", "departments": ["Linguistics and Philosophy", "Science, Technology, and Society"], "academic_levels": ["Undergraduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Caspar Hare", "Prof. David Jones"], "subject_primary": "Business Ethics", "subject_secondary": ["Health and Medicine", "Public Health", "Humanities", "Philosophy", "Ethics"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Business", "subtopics": [{"name": "Business Ethics", "specialties": []}]}, {"name": "Health and Medicine", "subtopics": [{"name": "Public Health", "specialties": []}]}, {"name": "Humanities", "subtopics": [{"name": "Philosophy", "specialties": ["Ethics"]}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Written Assignments"], "description": "This course does not seek to provide answers to ethical questions. Instead, the course hopes to teach students two things. First, how do you recognize ethical or moral problems in science and medicine? When something does not feel right (whether cloning, or failing to clone) — what exactly is the nature of the discomfort? What kind of tensions and conflicts exist within biomedicine? Second, how can you think productively about ethical and moral problems? What processes create them? Why do people disagree about them? How can an understanding of philosophy or history help resolve them? By the end of the course students will hopefully have sophisticated and nuanced ideas about problems in bioethics, even if they do not have comfortable answers.", "course_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/24-06j-bioethics-spring-2009/", "image_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/24-0